== C2a: one-sided P(max_{t<=tau} sigma W_t >= B) = erfc(B/(sigma sqrt(2 tau))) = 2 Phibar(1/z), z = sigma sqrt(tau)/B ==
  sigma=1 tau=1 B=1  z=1.000  exact=0.3173  MC=0.3187
  sigma=2 tau=0.25 B=1  z=1.000  exact=0.3173  MC=0.3181
  sigma=0.5 tau=4 B=1  z=1.000  exact=0.3173  MC=0.3165
  sigma=1 tau=1 B=2  z=0.500  exact=0.0455  MC=0.0453
  sigma=3 tau=1 B=2  z=1.500  exact=0.5050  MC=0.5015
  collapse: same z=0.5 from different (sigma,tau,B): [np.float64(0.0452), np.float64(0.0455), np.float64(0.0467)] exact 0.0455
== C2b: two-sided exit series (Feller) vs MC ==
  z=0.3: series=0.0017  MC=0.0018
  z=0.6: series=0.1912  MC=0.1899
  z=1.0: series=0.6292  MC=0.6272
  z=2.0: series=0.9908  MC=0.9907
== C2c: drift mu in the error (systematic bias): Bachelier-Levy  P = Phibar((B-mu t)/(s sqrt t)) + e^{2 mu B/s^2} Phibar((B+mu t)/(s sqrt t)) ==
  mu=-1.0: exact=0.0216 MC=0.0217   (second group Pe = mu B/s^2 = -1.5)
  mu=0.5: exact=0.2606 MC=0.2613   (second group Pe = mu B/s^2 = 0.75)
  mu=1.0: exact=0.4333 MC=0.4338   (second group Pe = mu B/s^2 = 1.5)
  same z, different Pe => different ruin: 0.1336 vs 0.4333  -> z alone is NOT sufficient once errors are biased
== C2d: hazard per unit time with resets every tau, in diffusion units B^2/sigma^2:  h B^2/sigma^2 = G(z) = -ln(1-P(z))/z^2 ==
  z      : [ 0.2  0.3  0.5  0.7  1.   1.5  2.   3.   5.  10.  30. ]
  G2(z)  : [0.     0.0191 0.3816 0.7461 0.9922 1.1263 1.1733 1.2069 1.224  1.2313
 1.2334]  (two-sided; limit pi^2/8 = 1.2337 )
  G1(z)  : [0.     0.0095 0.1863 0.3392 0.3817 0.3125 0.24   0.1492 0.0737 0.0253
 0.004 ]  (one-sided; ->0 as z->inf, non-monotone)
  => two-sided: for z >~ 1.5 hazard is within ~10% of pi^2 sigma^2/(8B^2): the fix interval stops mattering in the hot regime.
