  two-sided calendar-hazard elasticity d ln h/d ln tau at z=0.5: 1.490
  two-sided calendar-hazard elasticity d ln h/d ln tau at z=1: 0.243
  two-sided calendar-hazard elasticity d ln h/d ln tau at z=2: 0.051
  two-sided calendar-hazard elasticity d ln h/d ln tau at z=4: 0.012
== C2f: continuous correction (OU, rate 1/tau) instead of resets. Large-barrier MFPT T ~ tau sqrt(pi) z e^{1/z^2} ==
  sigma=1 tau=1 B=2.0 z=0.500: MFPT/tau=56.594  asymptote sqrt(pi) z e^(1/z^2)=48.386
  sigma=2 tau=0.25 B=2.0 z=0.500: MFPT/tau=56.594  asymptote sqrt(pi) z e^(1/z^2)=48.386
  sigma=1 tau=1 B=3.0 z=0.333: MFPT/tau=5118.673  asymptote sqrt(pi) z e^(1/z^2)=4787.447
  sigma=0.5 tau=4 B=1.5 z=0.667: MFPT/tau=12.928  asymptote sqrt(pi) z e^(1/z^2)=11.211
  (rows 1,2 share z and give identical MFPT/tau: the group sigma sqrt(tau)/B is forced by dimensional analysis)
== C2g: heavy-tailed jumps (compound Poisson, Pareto alpha=1.5 sizes, rate lam) + small diffusion: tau barely matters ==
  tau=0.25: per-window P=0.0060  calendar hazard=0.0241   single-big-jump rate lam*P(|J|>B)=0.0224
  tau=0.5: per-window P=0.0120  calendar hazard=0.0242   single-big-jump rate lam*P(|J|>B)=0.0224
  tau=1: per-window P=0.0257  calendar hazard=0.0260   single-big-jump rate lam*P(|J|>B)=0.0224
  tau=2: per-window P=0.0571  calendar hazard=0.0294   single-big-jump rate lam*P(|J|>B)=0.0224
  tau=4: per-window P=0.1320  calendar hazard=0.0354   single-big-jump rate lam*P(|J|>B)=0.0224
  => calendar-hazard elasticity wrt tau ~0.14 here (vs 1.5-12 for diffusive errors at z<=0.5): failure is set by the jump tail, not by sqrt(tau).
== C2h: long-memory shocks (fractional BM, H=0.75): the group is sigma tau^H / B, not sigma sqrt(tau)/B ==
  (sigma,tau)=(1,1): 0.0766;  (4^-H,4) same sigma tau^H: 0.0768;  (0.5,4) same sigma sqrt(tau): 0.1704
== C2i: fixes that don't fully reset (error multiplied by (1-k) at each fix): stationary pre-fix variance sigma^2 tau/(k(2-k)) ==
  k=1.0: calendar hazard=0.00008   z_eff=0.250; full-reset hazard at sigma=z_eff: 0.00010
  k=0.5: calendar hazard=0.00076   z_eff=0.289; full-reset hazard at sigma=z_eff: 0.00075
  k=0.2: calendar hazard=0.01324   z_eff=0.417; full-reset hazard at sigma=z_eff: 0.02629
  => with partial fixes the index needs roughly tau/(k(2-k)) (approximation good to ~2x at k=0.2): measured 'time to close' is not the variable the index needs.
== C2j: units-free consistency test against C3 (896 projects). In the reset model the per-window ruin is h*tau = -ln S(z), so h and tau alone pin z ==
  C3 coding A: hazard h = 0.0712/yr (137 events / 896, 2.33-yr window; literal coding 105 events gives 0.0535)
  tau=   7 d: implied z=0.294; predicted hazard elasticity d ln h/d ln tau = 5.21 -> HR per doubling of tau = 37.01; observed upper CI 1.087 caps the diffusive share of hazard at 0.2%
  tau=  44 d: implied z=0.350; predicted hazard elasticity d ln h/d ln tau = 3.52 -> HR per doubling of tau = 11.48; observed upper CI 1.087 caps the diffusive share of hazard at 0.8%
  tau= 180 d: implied z=0.420; predicted hazard elasticity d ln h/d ln tau = 2.29 -> HR per doubling of tau = 4.89; observed upper CI 1.087 caps the diffusive share of hazard at 2.2%
  tau= 546 d: implied z=0.511; predicted hazard elasticity d ln h/d ln tau = 1.41 -> HR per doubling of tau = 2.65; observed upper CI 1.087 caps the diffusive share of hazard at 5.2%
  => if dormancy were diffusive first passage reset every 'time to close', slow fixers would die several-fold faster. C3 rules that out:
     at most a few percent of dormancy hazard can come from that mechanism (conditional on tau measuring the fix interval).
